WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.100
Nothing can break the bond between one of
Game of Thrones' most memorable couples.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:08.590
Jason Momoa, who played Khal Drogo on the
first season of the HBO fantasy drama, got

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:12.629
emotional when discussing his on-screen wife
Emilia Clarke's health battle that almost

00:00:12.629 --> 00:00:14.269
took her life… twice.

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:18.300
The Daenerys Targaryen actress revealed earlier
this year that she had previously suffered

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.600
two brain aneurysms, the first of which came
in 2011, just as Game of Thrones was taking

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:23.600
off.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:27.980
Speaking with ET at the Game of Thrones Season
8 premiere in April, Momoa praised Clarke's

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.660
courage and tenacity during the hardest time
in her life.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.420
"It's very sad, because we almost lost her
numerous times.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:39.600
So, I love her to bits and she's here and
she's going to do great things with it and

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.600
teach the world."

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.780
In March of 2019, Clarke penned a moving essay
for the New Yorker in which she detailed that

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:50.180
she had experienced a brain aneurysm at the
age of 24, and then was stuck with a brain

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.970
bleed two years later while doctors attempted
to reduce the size of the original aneurysm.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:59.670
The actress opened up about the emergency
surgery she underwent to seal off the aneurysm.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.820
Clarke then detailed the second operation
required to "take care of" the expanding growth

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:08.290
in her brain plus the subsequent surgery needed
when that operation failed and left her with

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:09.280
a "massive bleed."

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:09.920
She wrote,

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.700
Of her second and third surgeries, she wrote,

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:51.070
In the midst of all this hardship and a double-brush
with death, Clarke was actively filming Game

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:55.979
of Thrones, as well as rehearsing for the
2013 Broadway rendition of Breakfast at Tiffany's.

00:01:55.979 --> 00:01:59.800
Though she kept her composure, told virtually
no one about what she had been through, and

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.869
even denied the genuinely accurate reports
that she had experienced life-threatening

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:06.660
trauma, Clarke was overwhelmed with exhaustion
and anxiety.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:07.660
She wrote,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.500
But, like the fireproof Mother of Dragons
she plays, Clarke was resilient and determined,

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.430
and came out on the other side with a renewed
sense of hope and purpose.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.020
After bravely sharing her story with the public,
Clarke announced that she had partnered with

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:40.959
executives in the United States and the United
Kingdom to develop a charity called SameYou,

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:44.870
which works to provide additional and more
immediate rehabilitation options for young

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:49.270
people who have suffered brain injuries, as
well as to fund research, advocate for policy

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:52.580
change, and "facilitate innovations in specialist
nurse training."

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.360
"She's so brave, in helping the world and
trying to raise awareness."

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.160
Six seasons later, Clarke says she couldn't
be more grateful to have "cheated death twice"

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.400
and lived to help bring Game of Thrones to
its epic conclusion.

